cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "uyghur-headword-lists: headword inventories from three printed Uyghur dictionaries"
message: "If you use this dataset, please cite it."
type: dataset
# Zenodo's CFF parser rejects a person with only `alias:` and fails silently
# (the release publishes, but no DOI is minted). A person with given-names and
# family-names is accepted; the entity is listed alongside as the maintainer.
authors:
  - given-names: UyghurReferenceTexts
    family-names: East Turkistan
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0004-4633-0408"
  - name: UyghurReferenceTexts
    website: "https://github.com/UyghurReferenceTexts"
repository-code: "https://github.com/UyghurReferenceTexts/uyghur-headword-lists"
abstract: >-
  Open, machine-readable headword lists extracted from three printed Uyghur
  dictionaries: 130,218 written forms and 48,245 sub-entry phrases in the
  Uyghur (ئۇيغۇر) script. Every entry is traceable to the scanned page column it
  was extracted from and carries a per-round audit status recording whether a
  native speaker has verified it against the original scan. Orthography follows
  the 2009 Hazirqi zaman Uyghur edebiy tilining imla lughiti standard; the
  other sources are the six-volume Uyghur edebiy tilining izahliq lughiti
  (1990-1999) and the 2011 Hazirqi zaman Uyghur tilining izahliq lughiti
  (qisqartilmisi). Chinese transliterations tagged in the sources' own
  etymology columns were reviewed individually and removed, with the full
  removal list published for audit. Also includes a benchmark quantifying
  frontier-model reliability on post-OCR correction of Uyghur Arabic script.
  Intended for spellchecking, orthographic normalization, OCR ground truth, and
  language preservation.
keywords:
  - Uyghur
  - lexicography
  - orthography
  - imla
  - wordlist
  - optical character recognition
  - low-resource languages
  - language preservation
  - linguistics
license: CC-BY-4.0
license-url: "https://github.com/UyghurReferenceTexts/uyghur-headword-lists/blob/main/LICENSE"
